## What I changed

The `membershipTier` field existed only in the acceptance spec — it wasn't in the schema, model, or mapper. I wired it in at the two natural layers:

1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** — added a `membershipTier` property to the `Owner` schema: a read-only string enum (`SILVER` / `BRONZE`). The OpenAPI generator produces `OwnerDto.MembershipTierEnum` from this.

2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** — added a `@Mapping(target = "membershipTier", expression = "java(membershipTier(owner))")` and a `membershipTier(Owner)` default method implementing the rule:

```java
default OwnerDto.MembershipTierEnum membershipTier(Owner owner) {
    Integer namesakeCount = owner.getNamesakeCount();
    boolean unique = namesakeCount != null && namesakeCount == 0;
    boolean hasEmail = owner.getEmail() != null && !owner.getEmail().isBlank();
    return unique && hasEmail ? OwnerDto.MembershipTierEnum.SILVER
            : OwnerDto.MembershipTierEnum.BRONZE;
}
```

This follows the same derived-field pattern already used for `displayName`, `initials`, and `membershipNumber`, so the GET-owner pipeline (`RespondWithOwner` → `ownerMapper.toOwnerDto`) picks it up with no YAML changes. `SILVER` requires `namesakeCount == 0` **and** an email present; everything else (namesakes exist, or no email) is `BRONZE`.

